1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,841
Ancient philosophy is not just about talking or lecturing,

2
00:00:06,841 --> 00:00:09,710
or even reading long, dense books.

3
00:00:09,710 --> 00:00:15,975
In fact, it is something people have used throughout history – to solve their problems

4
00:00:15,975 --> 00:00:19,870
and to achieve their greatest triumphs.

5
00:00:19,870 --> 00:00:24,282
Specifically, I am referring to Stoicism,

6
00:00:24,282 --> 00:00:29,054
which, in my opinion, is the most practical of all philosophies

7
00:00:29,054 --> 00:00:31,260
and therefore the most appealing.

8
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:37,994
Stoicism was founded in Ancient Greece by Zeno of Citium in the early 3rd

9
00:00:37,994 --> 00:00:39,116
century BC,

10
00:00:39,116 --> 00:00:46,130
but was practised by the likes of Epictetus, Cato, Seneca and Marcus Aurelius.

11
00:00:46,130 --> 00:00:52,195
Amazingly, we still have access to these ideas, despite the fact that the most

12
00:00:52,195 --> 00:00:55,810
famous Stoics never wrote anything down for publication.

13
00:00:55,810 --> 00:00:58,030
Cato definitely didn't.

14
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:03,790
Marcus Aurelius never intended his Meditations to be anything but personal.

15
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:11,361
Seneca's letters were, well, letters and Epictetus' thoughts come to us by way of

16
00:01:11,361 --> 00:01:13,490
a note-taking student.

17
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:21,971
Stoic principles were based on the idea that its followers could have an unshakable happiness in this life

18
00:01:21,971 --> 00:01:25,380
and the key to achieving this was virtue.

19
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:33,253
The road to virtue, in turn, lay in understanding that destructive emotions, like anger

20
00:01:33,253 --> 00:01:39,424
and jealousy, are under our conscious control – they don't have to control us,

21
00:01:39,424 --> 00:01:42,950
because we can learn to control them.

22
00:01:42,950 --> 00:01:45,400
In the words of Epictetus:

23
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:48,292
“external events I cannot control,

24
00:01:48,292 --> 00:01:52,690
but the choices I make with regard to them, I do control”.

25
00:01:52,690 --> 00:02:01,199
The modern day philosopher and writer Nassim Nicholas Taleb defines a Stoic as someone

26
00:02:01,199 --> 00:02:06,504
who has a different perspective on experiences which most of us would see as

27
00:02:06,504 --> 00:02:07,806
wholly negative;

28
00:02:07,806 --> 00:02:18,176
a Stoic “transforms fear into caution, pain into transformation, mistakes into initiation

29
00:02:18,176 --> 00:02:21,600
and desire into undertaking”.

30
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:28,714
Using this definition as a model, we can see that throughout the centuries Stoicism

31
00:02:28,714 --> 00:02:41,920
has been practised in more recent history by kings, presidents, artists, writers and entrepreneurs.

32
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:46,340
The founding fathers of the United States were inspired by the philosophy.

33
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:52,421
George Washington was introduced to Stoicism by his neighbours at age seventeen,

34
00:02:52,421 --> 00:02:57,458
and later, put on a play based on the life of Cato to inspire

35
00:02:57,458 --> 00:02:58,180
his men.

36
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:02,730
Thomas Jefferson kept a copy of Seneca beside his bed.

37
00:03:02,730 --> 00:03:08,150
Writers and artists have also been inspired by the stoics.

38
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:15,972
Eugène Delacroix, the renowned French Romantic artist (known best for his painting Liberty Leading

39
00:03:15,972 --> 00:03:22,250
the People) was an ardent Stoic, referring to it as his “consoling religion”.

40
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:31,268
The economist Adam Smith's theories on capitalism were significantly influenced by the Stoicism that

41
00:03:31,268 --> 00:03:36,980
he studied as a schoolboy, under a teacher who had translated Marcus Aurelius' works.

42
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:43,948
Today's political leaders are no different, with many finding their inspiration from the ancient

43
00:03:43,948 --> 00:03:44,450
texts.

44
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:51,150
Former US president Bill Clinton rereads Marcus Aurelius every single year,

45
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:57,670
and many have compared former President Obama's calm leadership style to that of Cato.

46
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:06,656
Wen Jiabao, the former prime minister of China, claims that Meditations is one of two books he travels with

47
00:04:06,656 --> 00:04:11,169
and that he has read it more than one hundred times over the course

48
00:04:11,169 --> 00:04:11,690
of his life.

49
00:04:11,690 --> 00:04:16,624
Stoicism had a profound influence on Albert Ellis,

50
00:04:16,624 --> 00:04:19,372
who invented Cognitive Behaviour Therapy,

51
00:04:19,372 --> 00:04:24,427
which is used to help people manage their problems by changing the way that they think

52
00:04:24,427 --> 00:04:25,550
and behave.

53
00:04:25,550 --> 00:04:29,440
It's most commonly used to treat depression.

54
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:34,375
The idea is that we can take control of our lives by challenging the

55
00:04:34,375 --> 00:04:42,360
irrational beliefs that create our faulty thinking, symptoms and behaviours by using logic instead.

56
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:47,200
Stoicism has also become popular in the world of business.

57
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:53,242
Stoic principles can build the resilience and state of mind required to overcome setbacks

58
00:04:53,242 --> 00:04:58,240
because Stoics teach turning obstacles into opportunity.

59
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:02,020
A lesson every business entrepreneur needs to learn.

60
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:09,784
I would argue that studying Stoicism is as relevant today as it was 2,000

61
00:05:09,784 --> 00:05:15,140
years ago, thanks to its brilliant insights into how to lead a good life.

62
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:19,939
At the very root of the thinking, there is a very simple way of

63
00:05:19,939 --> 00:05:25,260
living – control what you can and accept what you can't.

64
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:31,147
This is not as easy as it sounds and will require considerable practice –

65
00:05:31,147 --> 00:05:33,960
it can take a lifetime to master.

66
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:39,691
The Stoics also believed the most important foundation for a good

67
00:05:39,691 --> 00:05:44,401
and happy life is not money, fame, power or pleasure,

68
00:05:44,401 --> 00:05:51,715
but having a disciplined and principled character – something which seems to resonate with

69
00:05:51,715 --> 00:05:54,000
many people today.
